use "testing" ;

\ The module-deps template caches a module's deps-and-words frame. It never needs invalidation:
\ a module is immutable after its template is built. Reload creates a fresh module, and a runtime
\ redefinition writes to the dictionary or a local frame, never to module.words. These tests lock
\ that invariant in place.

\ Reload returns a fresh module and does not overwrite the cache entry the original binding points
\ at. The fixture content is fixed, so a content-divergence check is not expressible here: `use` is
\ parse-time and cannot take a runtime path, and there is no runtime way to call a word on a loaded
\ module value. This section asserts the original module's template is untouched by a reload.
"reload leaves the original module's template intact" test-section

orig: "./module_deps_invalidation_lib.1z" load ;
5 orig.compute 15 "original module word resolves through its template" assert=
"./module_deps_invalidation_lib.1z" reload drop
5 orig.compute 15 "original template survives a reload of the same file" assert=

"reloaded module resolves through a fresh template" test-section

use "./module_deps_invalidation_lib.1z" reload shadow-ok ;
5 compute 15 "reloaded module word resolves through its fresh template" assert=

"top-level redefinition does not leak into a module's template" test-section

report "module-helper" "module word uses its own helper" assert=
helper: override ( -- s ) [ "top-helper" ] ;
helper "top-helper" "redefinition is in effect at top level" assert=
report "module-helper" "module word still uses its own helper after redefine" assert=
